<body>
<h1>Patient Vault Detailed Legal and HIPAA Policy Packet</h1>
<p style='text-align:center'><strong>Prepared for attorney review only</strong><br>Patient Vault / Modern Medicine<br>Prepared July 15, 2026</p>
<div class='notice'><strong>Important:</strong> This is a detailed working draft for legal review. It is not legal advice and should not be adopted, published, or distributed as a final policy until reviewed and approved by qualified healthcare counsel.</div>
<h2>1. Purpose and Attorney Review Instructions</h2><p>This document is a comprehensive draft policy packet for Patient Vault and Modern Medicine. It is intended to be reviewed by healthcare counsel before it is adopted, distributed, or relied upon. The document is intentionally written in practical operational language so that legal counsel can revise it into final policies, a compliance manual, patient-facing notices, workforce procedures, and vendor contract requirements.</p><p>Patient Vault is a cloud-hosted electronic medical record and clinic operations system. It may create, receive, maintain, and transmit electronic protected health information. Because of that, the organization must treat this document as only one part of a broader HIPAA compliance program. Technical safeguards, contracts, workforce training, risk analysis, incident response, and ongoing monitoring all need to work together.</p><p>Counsel should confirm whether Patient Vault is being used only by the medical practice, offered to other clinics as software, or operated by a separate legal entity. That distinction matters because the legal roles may change. If Patient Vault is operated by a separate company for the benefit of a covered entity, counsel should determine whether that entity is acting as a business associate and whether a business associate agreement is needed between the practice and the software operator.</p><p>Nothing in this document should be treated as final legal advice. The placeholders in brackets should be completed by counsel or compliance leadership. State-specific rules, payer rules, professional licensing rules, prescribing rules, telehealth rules, and medical board rules must be checked before use.</p><h2>2. System Description</h2><p>Patient Vault is a web-based clinical application used for patient registration, scheduling, charting, clinical notes, document storage, internal messages, forms, reminders, and administrative user management. The production application is available at app.patientvault.care over HTTPS.</p><p>The production infrastructure currently uses AWS Lightsail for the application server, AWS RDS PostgreSQL for the database, and AWS S3 for document storage. Documents stored in S3 are not public. The S3 bucket has public access blocked, versioning enabled, and server-side encryption enabled. The database is hosted on AWS RDS with encryption and automated backups.</p><p>The system includes role-based access control, unique user accounts, password policy enforcement, multi-factor authentication support, account lockout after repeated failed logins, audit logging, secure cookies, a five-minute idle logout, HTTPS, security headers, and login rate limiting. These safeguards are designed to support HIPAA-oriented security, but counsel should verify that the technical and administrative controls match the organization?s risk analysis and operating model.</p><p>Patient Vault may later connect to outside services such as OpenAI or another AI vendor, transcription vendors, fax vendors, and e-prescribing vendors. No vendor that receives protected health information should be used until the organization has signed an appropriate business associate agreement and configured the service according to the vendor?s HIPAA-eligible requirements.</p><h2>3. Data Classification and PHI Handling</h2><p>Patient Vault may contain protected health information including patient names, demographics, contact information, medical record numbers, appointment information, clinical notes, diagnoses, medications, uploaded records, referral documents, forms, internal messages, and audit records that identify who accessed patient information. When any of this information relates to an identifiable patient, it must be treated as PHI or electronic PHI.</p><p>Users must handle all patient chart content as confidential medical information. PHI must not be copied into personal email, personal cloud storage, consumer AI tools, personal devices, unapproved messaging apps, or unapproved external services. PHI should only be accessed when the user has a legitimate work-related reason connected to treatment, payment, healthcare operations, or another legally permitted purpose.</p><p>The organization should classify data into at least three categories. First, PHI and ePHI, which require the strongest protection. Second, confidential business information, such as contracts, pricing, security procedures, and internal policies. Third, public or low-risk information, such as general website content that does not identify patients. Staff should be trained that most information inside Patient Vault is either PHI or confidential business information.</p><p>Developers and administrators must not intentionally place PHI into source code, environment variable names, debugging output, browser local storage, public issue trackers, public repositories, screenshots, or unsecured support messages. Troubleshooting should use resource IDs, timestamps, and non-PHI technical details whenever possible.</p><h2>4. HIPAA Privacy Rule Policy</h2><p>The organization may use and disclose PHI only as permitted by HIPAA, applicable state law, and organizational policy. Routine uses may include treatment, payment, healthcare operations, patient access requests, care coordination, quality review, scheduling, and administrative operations. Any use outside those categories should be reviewed by the Privacy Officer or legal counsel before the disclosure occurs.</p><p>The minimum necessary standard applies to most uses and disclosures. Workforce members should access only the patient information needed to perform their assigned duties. Clinicians may need broad access for treatment, but staff, billing users, administrative users, and readonly users should receive only the access necessary for their roles. Patient Vault?s role-based permissions are intended to support this principle, but role assignments must still be reviewed by management.</p><p>The organization should maintain a Notice of Privacy Practices for patients. Counsel should prepare or approve the final notice. The notice should explain how PHI may be used and disclosed, patient rights, complaint procedures, the organization?s duties, and contact information for privacy questions. The notice should be made available to patients according to HIPAA and state requirements.</p><p>Patients may have rights to access their records, request amendments, request restrictions, request confidential communications, receive an accounting of disclosures, and file complaints. Patient Vault can support these workflows by retrieving chart records, documents, and audit logs, but the legal procedure and response deadlines must be defined by counsel. Staff should not deny or delay patient rights requests without Privacy Officer review.</p><p>The organization must prohibit retaliation against a patient or workforce member who files a privacy complaint, participates in an investigation, or exercises rights under HIPAA. Complaints should be logged, investigated, and resolved under the supervision of the Privacy Officer.</p><h2>5. HIPAA Security Rule Administrative Safeguards</h2><p>The Security Officer should maintain a written risk analysis that identifies reasonably anticipated threats to the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of ePHI. The risk analysis should cover application hosting, database storage, document storage, user access, remote access, endpoints, backups, vendors, audit logs, incident response, AI integrations, fax integrations, transcription integrations, and e-prescribing integrations.</p><p>A risk management plan should document how identified risks are addressed. Some risks may be reduced through technical safeguards such as MFA, encryption, backups, and audit logging. Other risks require administrative safeguards, such as training, access reviews, sanctions, vendor contracts, and written procedures. Counsel should verify that the risk analysis and risk management plan are retained as compliance records.</p><p>Workforce security procedures should define how access is granted, changed, and removed. New users should receive access only after authorization by management. Role changes should be documented. Terminated users should be disabled promptly, and active sessions should be revoked. Administrative access should be limited to the smallest number of people necessary.</p><p>Security awareness training should be provided before system access is granted and at least annually. Training should cover phishing, password management, MFA, workstation locking, reporting incidents, minimum necessary access, appropriate use of Patient Vault, and the prohibition on entering PHI into unapproved AI or vendor systems.</p><p>The organization should maintain a contingency plan that includes backups, disaster recovery procedures, emergency mode operations, and periodic testing. A technical backup existing in AWS is not enough by itself; the organization should know who is responsible for restoration, how long recovery should take, and how clinical operations will continue during downtime.</p><h2>6. HIPAA Security Rule Physical Safeguards</h2><p>Although Patient Vault is cloud-hosted, physical safeguards still matter because workforce members access ePHI from computers, phones, tablets, and office workstations. The organization should require users to prevent unauthorized viewing of patient information on screens and printed materials. Workstations should lock automatically when unattended, and users should manually lock screens when stepping away.</p><p>Devices used to access Patient Vault should be protected with operating system passwords, current security updates, antivirus or endpoint protection where appropriate, and encryption where possible. Personal devices should not be used for PHI unless the organization has approved a bring-your-own-device policy that addresses encryption, lock screens, remote wipe, and reporting of lost or stolen devices.</p><p>Printed PHI should be minimized. When printing is necessary, documents should be retrieved promptly from printers, stored securely, and shredded or destroyed when no longer needed. Fax cover sheets, referral packets, and printed notes should not be left in public areas or unattended exam rooms.</p><p>Office access should be controlled so that unauthorized individuals cannot view patient records, access workstations, or remove devices. Visitors, contractors, and cleaning staff should not have unsupervised access to areas where PHI is visible or accessible unless appropriate safeguards are in place.</p><h2>7. HIPAA Security Rule Technical Safeguards</h2><p>Patient Vault uses unique user accounts so activity can be attributed to individual users. Shared accounts should not be used for routine operations. If emergency access procedures are needed, counsel and the Security Officer should define how emergency access is granted, logged, reviewed, and terminated.</p><p>Authentication is supported through passwords and MFA. The organization should require MFA for administrators, clinicians, and any user with access to PHI. Passwords should be at least twelve characters and include uppercase letters, lowercase letters, numbers, and special characters. Passwords should be changed after administrative reset, and users should not reuse passwords from other systems.</p><p>The application has a five-minute idle logout. This helps reduce the risk of unattended workstation access. Users should still lock their devices manually when stepping away. The five-minute timeout should be reviewed with clinicians to confirm it supports workflow without encouraging unsafe workarounds.</p><p>Audit logs should record PHI access, note creation and updates, document uploads and downloads, login events, failed login events, MFA events, user administration events, and exports such as printable note views. Audit logs should contain user IDs, patient IDs, resource IDs, timestamps, IP addresses where available, and user agents where available. Audit metadata should avoid raw PHI whenever possible.</p><p>Transmission security is provided through HTTPS. Cookies are configured for secure session handling in production. The application should not send API secrets to the browser. Any future third-party integration must be reviewed to confirm that PHI is transmitted only over encrypted connections and only to approved HIPAA-eligible services under BAA.</p><h2>8. User Access and Account Management Procedure</h2><p>Before creating a new user, management should verify the person?s identity, role, employment or contractor status, and need for access. The administrator should assign the least privileged role that supports the person?s duties. Users should not be given administrator access merely for convenience.</p><p>When a temporary password is issued, the user should be required to change it at first login. MFA should be enabled before the user begins accessing real patient charts. Backup codes or MFA recovery procedures should be stored securely and should not be shared by email or text unless counsel and security leadership approve the method.</p><p>User access should be reviewed at least quarterly. The review should confirm that each user still works with the organization, still needs Patient Vault access, has the correct role, and has MFA enabled. Any unnecessary accounts should be disabled. Administrative accounts should receive special review.</p><p>When a user leaves the organization or no longer needs access, the account should be disabled immediately. Active sessions should be revoked. If the user had access to vendor portals, API keys, shared inboxes, or other systems, those credentials should also be reviewed. Recent audit logs should be reviewed if there is concern about inappropriate access.</p><h2>9. Audit Log Review Procedure</h2><p>Audit logs are a critical safeguard but only if they are reviewed when appropriate. The Security Officer or designee should review audit logs after suspected inappropriate access, after an incident report, before responding to certain patient requests, and during periodic compliance checks.</p><p>Routine audit review may include checking for unusual access patterns, access outside normal hours, repeated failed logins, locked accounts, downloads of multiple documents, access by users who are leaving the organization, and administrative changes to user accounts. The goal is not to accuse users, but to detect mistakes, misuse, compromised credentials, and workflow problems.</p><p>Audit log review should be documented. Documentation should include who performed the review, when it was performed, what period was reviewed, what issue was investigated, what records were checked, and what conclusion was reached. If inappropriate access is suspected, the matter should be escalated to the Privacy Officer, Security Officer, and legal counsel.</p><h2>10. Data Retention and Destruction Policy</h2><p>Medical record retention must be determined by applicable state law, federal law, payer requirements, professional standards, and organizational policy. Counsel should insert the correct retention period for the clinic?s state and specialty. Until counsel confirms otherwise, Patient Vault should favor retention and archival rather than permanent deletion.</p><p>Patient records, notes, documents, forms, messages related to care, schedules, and audit logs should not be deleted casually. If a record is incorrect, the preferred approach is usually correction, addendum, or archival rather than silent deletion. Permanent deletion should require authorization and documentation.</p><p>When destruction is legally permitted, the organization should document the basis for destruction, the records destroyed, the date of destruction, the person authorizing destruction, and the method used. Backups may retain deleted data until backup retention periods expire. Counsel should determine whether backup retention practices satisfy legal requirements.</p><p>Audit logs and compliance documentation should generally be retained for a conservative period. HIPAA documentation is commonly retained for at least six years, but state medical record retention periods may be longer. Counsel should specify the final retention schedule.</p><h2>11. Backup and Disaster Recovery Policy</h2><p>Patient Vault currently uses AWS RDS automated backups with seven-day retention and encrypted storage. Documents are stored in S3 with encryption, public access blocked, and versioning enabled. These controls reduce the risk of data loss but should be supported by written restore procedures and periodic testing.</p><p>The Security Officer should verify backup status at least monthly and after major infrastructure changes. Verification should include confirming that database snapshots exist, that backups are encrypted, that document storage remains private, and that production environment variables still point to the expected services.</p><p>A restore test should be performed at least annually. A restore test should confirm that a database snapshot can be restored to a test environment, that the application can connect to the restored database, and that representative records can be accessed. If S3 documents are part of the restore scenario, representative documents should also be tested.</p><p>The organization should define a recovery time objective and recovery point objective. For example, leadership should decide how long the clinic can operate without Patient Vault and how much data loss is acceptable. Those targets should be realistic for the current AWS setup and staffing model.</p><h2>12. Incident Response Policy</h2><p>All workforce members must report suspected privacy or security incidents promptly. Examples include lost devices, suspicious login activity, phishing, incorrect patient documents, misdirected faxes, unauthorized chart access, malware, ransomware, accidental disclosure, vendor incidents, and compromised passwords.</p><p>The first response should be containment. Depending on the incident, containment may include disabling accounts, revoking sessions, rotating passwords or API keys, isolating devices, stopping a vendor integration, preserving logs, or temporarily disabling a feature. Staff should avoid deleting evidence unless directed by the Security Officer or counsel.</p><p>The Privacy Officer and Security Officer should determine whether PHI was involved, what information was affected, who accessed or received it, whether it was actually viewed or acquired, and what mitigation occurred. If a breach may have occurred, legal counsel should guide the risk assessment and notification analysis.</p><p>HIPAA breach notification has strict timing requirements, including a general outer deadline of sixty days for certain notifications. State law may require faster notice. The organization should maintain an incident response contact list that includes leadership, legal counsel, IT support, cyber insurance contact, and any key vendors.</p><h2>13. Vendor and Business Associate Agreement Policy</h2><p>Before any vendor creates, receives, maintains, or transmits PHI on behalf of the organization, the vendor relationship must be reviewed. If the vendor is a business associate, a signed business associate agreement must be in place before PHI is shared. The BAA should be reviewed by counsel.</p><p>AWS should be handled through AWS Artifact by accepting the AWS Business Associate Addendum for the account that hosts Patient Vault. Counsel or the Security Officer should confirm that all AWS services used for PHI are HIPAA-eligible services and are configured securely. The BAA does not by itself make the system compliant; the organization remains responsible for configuration and use.</p><p>OpenAI or another AI vendor should not receive PHI until a BAA is signed and the account is configured for HIPAA-eligible API use. The organization should confirm data retention, model training, logging, abuse monitoring, and endpoint eligibility. Consumer AI subscriptions should not be used with PHI.</p><p>Fax, transcription, and e-prescribing vendors also require review. The organization should confirm whether the vendor signs a BAA, whether the specific product tier is covered, what subcontractors are used, where data is stored, how long data is retained, how audit logs are made available, and how incidents are reported.</p><h2>14. AI Use Policy</h2><p>AI features may provide drafting, summarization, organization, or administrative assistance, but they do not replace clinician judgment. Any AI-generated content that enters the chart should be reviewed by an appropriate human user before it is saved, signed, sent, or relied upon. Clinicians remain responsible for clinical decisions and documentation accuracy.</p><p>Users must not paste patient names, notes, documents, diagnoses, or other PHI into consumer AI tools. This includes ordinary ChatGPT accounts, personal AI assistants, browser extensions, and unapproved transcription tools. Only approved AI integrations configured under BAA may be used with PHI.</p><p>Approved AI workflows should follow the minimum necessary principle. Prompts should include only the information required for the task. AI outputs should be treated as draft content until reviewed. If AI output is materially changed before being saved, the final chart should reflect the clinician?s reviewed content rather than a hidden automated decision.</p><p>Counsel should decide whether patient-facing notices, consent language, or internal disclosures are needed for AI-assisted documentation. The organization should also decide whether AI activity should be separately labeled in audit logs or chart metadata.</p><h2>15. Fax Policy</h2><p>Faxing remains common in healthcare but creates disclosure risks. Before sending PHI by fax through Patient Vault or any connected vendor, the organization should sign a BAA with the fax vendor and approve a fax procedure. Staff should verify recipient numbers before sending and should use cover sheets where appropriate.</p><p>Misdirected faxes should be treated as potential privacy incidents. Staff should report the event immediately, attempt to contact the recipient, request destruction or return of the information, document what was sent, and escalate to the Privacy Officer. Legal counsel should determine whether the incident is reportable.</p><p>Fax logs should be retained according to policy. Logs should record sender, recipient, time, status, patient or document reference, and failure information where available. Failed or partial transmissions should be reviewed and corrected promptly.</p><h2>16. Transcription Policy</h2><p>Before transcription is enabled, the organization should determine whether audio recordings will be created, where they will be stored, how long they will be retained, and whether patient consent is required under state law. Some states have specific consent rules for recording conversations, even in clinical settings.</p><p>A transcription vendor must sign a BAA before receiving audio or transcript content containing PHI. The organization should understand whether the vendor uses humans, AI models, subcontractors, or offshore processing. Counsel should review whether any additional patient notice or consent is appropriate.</p><p>Transcripts should be treated as draft clinical documentation until reviewed. The clinician or authorized user should verify accuracy before the transcript becomes part of the medical record. The system should avoid storing unnecessary raw audio longer than needed unless counsel approves the retention policy.</p><h2>17. E-Prescribing Policy</h2><p>Patient Vault should not attempt to send real prescriptions to pharmacies without an approved e-prescribing vendor. Pharmacy network access, controlled substance prescribing, prescriber identity proofing, DEA requirements, NPI requirements, state license requirements, and EPCS rules are specialized regulatory and vendor processes.</p><p>Before enabling e-prescribing, the organization should select a vendor that offers a BAA and supports the required prescribing workflows. Prescribers may need identity proofing and enrollment. Controlled substances may require additional EPCS setup, two-factor authentication, and compliance with DEA rules.</p><p>The final prescribing workflow should define who may prescribe, who may prepare prescriptions for clinician review, how allergies and medications are checked, how pharmacies are selected, how failed transmissions are handled, how cancellations and renewals are handled, and how prescription activity is documented in the medical record.</p><p>Counsel should review any vendor contract, BAA, indemnity language, audit rights, data retention terms, and responsibility allocation before the integration is activated.</p><h2>18. Workforce Training Policy</h2><p>Training should be completed before a workforce member receives access to Patient Vault. Training should cover the organization?s privacy and security policies, permitted uses of PHI, minimum necessary access, user account responsibilities, password rules, MFA, incident reporting, workstation security, phishing, document handling, and restrictions on AI and external vendors.</p><p>Annual refresher training should be required. Additional training should occur when policies change, when new features are added, after an incident, or when audit review shows a workflow problem. Training should be practical and tied to how staff actually use Patient Vault.</p><p>Training records should be retained. Records should include the trainee?s name, date, topics covered, trainer or training system, and attestation. Counsel should specify retention requirements for training records.</p><h2>19. Sanctions Policy</h2><p>The organization should maintain and apply a sanctions policy for workforce members who violate privacy or security policies. Sanctions may include verbal warning, written warning, retraining, access suspension, termination of access, employment discipline, termination, or reporting to licensing boards or authorities when required.</p><p>Sanctions should be applied consistently and proportionately. Factors may include intent, harm, prior history, whether the person self-reported, whether PHI was disclosed, whether the conduct was reckless or malicious, and whether mitigation was possible. Legal counsel and HR should review serious cases.</p><p>The sanctions process should be documented. Documentation should include the violation, investigation summary, sanction imposed, date, responsible decision maker, and any retraining or corrective action required.</p><h2>20. Patient-Facing Documents Needed</h2><p>This policy packet is not a substitute for patient-facing legal documents. Counsel should prepare or approve a Notice of Privacy Practices, website privacy policy, website terms of use, patient consent forms, communication consent language, telehealth consent if applicable, AI disclosure language if applicable, and medical record request procedures.</p><p>If Patient Vault is eventually offered to other clinics, the business may also need customer-facing terms of service, a software subscription agreement, a BAA template between Patient Vault and customer clinics, support terms, service level terms, data export terms, termination assistance provisions, indemnity provisions, and limitations of liability.</p><h2>21. Practical Compliance Checklist</h2><p>Before using Patient Vault for real patient data at scale, leadership should confirm that the AWS BAA has been accepted, real users have unique accounts, MFA is enabled, demo passwords are changed or disabled, staff have completed HIPAA training, the privacy and security officers are named, incident response contacts are documented, backups are verified, and at least one restore test is scheduled.</p><p>Before enabling AI, fax, transcription, or e-prescribing, leadership should confirm that the vendor has signed a BAA, the exact product tier is covered, account settings are configured properly, staff are trained, audit logging is adequate, and counsel has reviewed the workflow.</p><p>Before sharing this system with outside clinics or customers, counsel should review corporate structure, covered entity and business associate roles, customer contracts, BAAs, support procedures, insurance, data ownership, data export, account termination, and breach responsibility allocation.</p><h2>22. Attorney Action Items</h2><p>Counsel should revise this document for the correct legal entity names, clinic ownership structure, HIPAA role, state retention periods, state breach notification deadlines, prescribing requirements, telehealth requirements, patient notice requirements, employee sanctions language, vendor contract language, and insurance requirements.</p><p>Counsel should also identify which parts of this document should become internal policy, which parts should become staff procedures, which parts should become patient-facing notices, and which parts should become vendor or customer contract terms. The final version should include approval signatures from the Privacy Officer, Security Officer, and business owner.</p>

<h2>Appendix A - Vendor Review Table</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Vendor Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>BAA Required Before PHI?</th><th>Attorney Review Focus</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cloud hosting</td><td>AWS Lightsail, RDS, S3</td><td>Yes</td><td>AWS Artifact BAA, HIPAA-eligible services, encryption, backups, shared responsibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI API</td><td>OpenAI or alternate AI vendor</td><td>Yes</td><td>Covered endpoints, data retention, model training, logging, patient disclosure, human review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fax API</td><td>Fax.Plus, eFax, other healthcare fax vendors</td><td>Yes</td><td>Misdirected fax workflow, audit logs, transmission security, retention</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transcription API</td><td>Deepgram, AWS Transcribe, other transcription vendors</td><td>Yes</td><td>Recording consent, audio retention, human review, subcontractors</td></tr>
<tr><td>E-prescribing</td><td>DoseSpot, ScriptSure, DrFirst</td><td>Yes</td><td>EPCS, DEA, NPI, identity proofing, pharmacy network, prescription audit trail</td></tr>
</table>
